    stem: "While electric and hybrid electric cars grow in popularity, two‐thirds of all new private cars licenced in Ireland between January and June 2021 were powered only by petrol or diesel. Petrol and diesel are mixtures of mostly hydrocarbons obtained from crude oil. Since July 2021, Dublin Bus has been testing hydrogen fuel‐cell electric double‐decker buses as part of Ireland’s climate action strategy to reduce greenhouse gas emissions. The overall fuel‐cell reaction is that hydrogen combines with oxygen to form water and energy is released in the form of an electric current. The balanced equation for the overall fuel‐cell reaction is: H₂ (g) + ½O₂ (g) → H₂O (l) Currently, steam reforming of natural gas is the source of most of the world’s hydrogen. The following is the balanced equation for the steam reforming of methane, an endothermic reaction: CH₄ (g) + H₂O (g) → CO (g) + 3H₂ (g) ΔH = 206.2 kJ",
