    id: "chem-2025-hl-q1-e-iv", topicId: "chem-4-1", conceptId: "percentage-by-mass-of-iron-in-a-tablet",
    section: "A", questionRef: "2025 HL Q1(e)(iv)",
    stem: "A student carried out an experiment to determine the iron(II) content of an iron supplement tablet. Six iron tablets with a total mass of 2.496 g were dissolved in dilute sulfuric acid. The solution and the washings were transferred to a 250 cm³ volumetric flask and made up to the mark using deionised water. A 25.0 cm³ portion of this solution was transferred into a conical flask and some sulfuric acid was added. The solution was then titrated against a previously standardised solution of potassium manganate(VII) (KMnO₄). A number of titrations were carried out. The titration reaction is described by the following balanced equation: MnO₄⁻ + 5Fe²⁺ + 8H⁺ → Mn²⁺ + 5Fe³⁺ + 4H₂O On average, 22.5 cm³ of 0.010 M potassium manganate(VII) solution were required for complete reaction with 25.0 cm³ of the solution of the iron(II) in excess acid.",
